1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:02,554
How much space is under this curve?

2
00:00:02,554 --> 00:00:06,908
There is no formula for "area" that
works on a wiggly shape directly.

3
00:00:06,908 --> 00:00:10,403
But there is a formula for
the area of a rectangle.

4
00:00:10,403 --> 00:00:13,468
So: fill the region with rectangles.

5
00:00:13,468 --> 00:00:15,441
Four of them get the rough idea,

6
00:00:15,441 --> 00:00:17,647
with obvious gaps and overhangs.

7
00:00:17,647 --> 00:00:20,805
Eight are better. Sixteen, forty,

8
00:00:20,805 --> 00:00:26,006
eighty. The errors shrink, and the total
settles towards one particular number.

9
00:00:26,006 --> 00:00:28,224
That is the whole idea of an integral,

10
00:00:28,224 --> 00:00:30,558
and it is a perfect thing to animate,

11
00:00:30,558 --> 00:00:32,775
because the important
part is not the answer.

12
00:00:32,775 --> 00:00:35,433
It's the motion of getting there.

13
00:00:35,433 --> 00:00:38,266
Before graphs, the simplest
coordinate system of all:

14
00:00:38,266 --> 00:00:40,112
a number line. You give it a range,

15
00:00:40,112 --> 00:00:42,550
a step between ticks
and a length on screen,

16
00:00:42,550 --> 00:00:44,210
and it can label its own ticks.

17
00:00:44,210 --> 00:00:46,311
A number line converts in both directions.

18
00:00:46,311 --> 00:00:48,354
Number to point, n two p for short,

19
00:00:48,354 --> 00:00:50,467
tells you where a number lives on screen.

20
00:00:50,467 --> 00:00:52,221
Point to number does the reverse.

21
00:00:52,221 --> 00:00:55,088
And these conversions follow
the line wherever it goes:

22
00:00:55,088 --> 00:00:56,771
rotate it, move it, shrink it,

23
00:00:56,771 --> 00:01:00,045
and two point five is still exactly
where two point five should be.

24
00:01:00,045 --> 00:01:03,853
We used n two p last episode to
park the pointer above a value.

25
00:01:03,853 --> 00:01:05,537
Now put two number lines together,

26
00:01:05,537 --> 00:01:07,928
one across and one up, crossing at zero.

27
00:01:07,928 --> 00:01:09,437
That, give or take some styling,

28
00:01:09,437 --> 00:01:11,700
is exactly what Axes are.

29
00:01:11,700 --> 00:01:15,113
To draw graphs, we need a second
kind of coordinate system,

30
00:01:15,113 --> 00:01:17,911
one that belongs to the
graph rather than the screen.

31
00:01:17,911 --> 00:01:19,479
That is what Axes are.

32
00:01:19,479 --> 00:01:22,834
You tell Axes the range of
numbers each axis should cover,

33
00:01:22,834 --> 00:01:24,715
and how long it should be on screen.

34
00:01:24,715 --> 00:01:27,583
This one runs from minus
three to three across,

35
00:01:27,583 --> 00:01:29,963
and from minus two to nine upwards,

36
00:01:29,963 --> 00:01:34,421
squeezed into a box seven
units wide and six units tall.

37
00:01:34,421 --> 00:01:36,790
Add coordinates puts numbers on the ticks.

38
00:01:36,790 --> 00:01:39,298
Now there are two ways
to describe a position:

39
00:01:39,298 --> 00:01:41,353
in graph numbers, or in screen units.

40
00:01:41,353 --> 00:01:44,569
The method c to p, short
for coordinates to point,

41
00:01:44,569 --> 00:01:46,124
converts from one to the other.

42
00:01:46,124 --> 00:01:48,876
Ask for the point two, four on this graph,

43
00:01:48,876 --> 00:01:52,127
and you get back the screen
location where that point is drawn.

44
00:01:52,127 --> 00:01:56,005
This matters because it means you
can think in the graph's own numbers.

45
00:01:56,005 --> 00:01:59,058
To put dots where the
line crosses the parabola,

46
00:01:59,058 --> 00:02:02,518
we solve x squared equals two x plus one,

47
00:02:02,518 --> 00:02:06,244
which gives one minus root
two and one plus root two,

48
00:02:06,244 --> 00:02:09,541
and let c to p work out where
those points are on screen.

49
00:02:09,541 --> 00:02:15,133
If you resize the axes later, the
dots still land in the right place.

50
00:02:15,133 --> 00:02:17,049
What does plot actually do?

51
00:02:17,049 --> 00:02:19,057
It can't draw a function exactly,

52
00:02:19,057 --> 00:02:21,786
any more than a computer
can store a perfect circle.

53
00:02:21,786 --> 00:02:25,965
Instead, it evaluates your function
at many x values across the range,

54
00:02:25,965 --> 00:02:29,820
and threads a smooth chain of
cubic curves through the results.

55
00:02:29,820 --> 00:02:31,468
Episode four in action.

56
00:02:31,468 --> 00:02:33,686
That works beautifully
for smooth functions.

57
00:02:33,686 --> 00:02:36,008
But it means plot
doesn't know about jumps.

58
00:02:36,008 --> 00:02:38,469
Plot one over x straight through zero,

59
00:02:38,469 --> 00:02:42,510
and the curve may join the two sides with
a steep line that isn't really there.

60
00:02:42,510 --> 00:02:45,308
The fix is to plot each side separately,

61
00:02:45,308 --> 00:02:48,617
or to tell plot where
the discontinuities are.

62
00:02:48,617 --> 00:02:51,415
Here is the opening scene.

63
00:02:51,415 --> 00:02:55,037
Axes have a method called
get Riemann rectangles,

64
00:02:55,037 --> 00:02:57,162
which builds the rectangles for you:

65
00:02:57,162 --> 00:03:00,517
give it a graph, a range, and a width d x.

66
00:03:00,517 --> 00:03:04,163
Each rectangle's height is the
function's value at its left edge,

67
00:03:04,163 --> 00:03:07,170
and its colour shifts gradually
from one end to the other,

68
00:03:07,170 --> 00:03:10,072
so you can still tell them
apart when there are dozens.

69
00:03:10,072 --> 00:03:14,948
To refine, we build a new set with a
smaller d x and transform the old set into

70
00:03:14,948 --> 00:03:17,549
it. Transform pairs up rectangles,

71
00:03:17,549 --> 00:03:19,615
and because the new set has more of them,

72
00:03:19,615 --> 00:03:21,392
the extra ones grow out of the old,

73
00:03:21,392 --> 00:03:23,598
which gives that lovely splitting motion.

74
00:03:23,598 --> 00:03:27,034
Finally, get area shades the
exact region under the curve,

75
00:03:27,034 --> 00:03:29,217
and we write down the exact answer:

76
00:03:29,217 --> 00:03:32,293
twenty two thirds, or about
seven point three three,

77
00:03:32,293 --> 00:03:36,500
which is the number the rectangles
were closing in on all along.

78
00:03:36,500 --> 00:03:38,857
Now let's make the boundary move.

79
00:03:38,857 --> 00:03:41,783
Store the right-hand edge of
the area in a value tracker,

80
00:03:41,783 --> 00:03:45,858
b, and always redraw
the area from zero to b.

81
00:03:45,858 --> 00:03:49,411
Then animate b, and the
area sweeps out behind it.

82
00:03:49,411 --> 00:03:53,288
The readout shows the integral
of sine from zero to b,

83
00:03:53,288 --> 00:03:56,365
which works out to one minus cosine of b.

84
00:03:56,365 --> 00:04:00,219
As b grows to pi, the
area grows to exactly two.

85
00:04:00,219 --> 00:04:02,321
Then something interesting happens.

86
00:04:02,321 --> 00:04:05,409
Past pi, the curve dips below the axis,

87
00:04:05,409 --> 00:04:08,520
and area below the axis
counts as negative.

88
00:04:08,520 --> 00:04:13,582
So the total starts falling, and
by two pi it is back to zero:

89
00:04:13,582 --> 00:04:17,181
the part below cancels
the part above exactly.

90
00:04:17,181 --> 00:04:20,548
This is the idea of an
integral as a running total,

91
00:04:20,548 --> 00:04:23,695
an accumulation. It's one
thing to be told that,

92
00:04:23,695 --> 00:04:27,133
and quite another to watch it happen.

93
00:04:27,200 --> 00:04:30,335
Area was the first big idea of calculus.

94
00:04:30,335 --> 00:04:31,519
Here is the second:

95
00:04:31,519 --> 00:04:34,027
the slope of a curve at a single point.

96
00:04:34,027 --> 00:04:35,896
A straight line has a slope:

97
00:04:35,896 --> 00:04:38,682
how far it rises for each step across.

98
00:04:38,682 --> 00:04:41,793
A curve doesn't, because it
keeps changing direction.

99
00:04:41,793 --> 00:04:44,243
But we can pick two points on the curve,

100
00:04:44,243 --> 00:04:46,553
draw the line through
them, called a secant,

101
00:04:46,553 --> 00:04:48,074
and measure its slope:

102
00:04:48,074 --> 00:04:51,395
the rise, d y, divided by the run,

103
00:04:51,395 --> 00:04:52,660
d x.

104
00:04:52,660 --> 00:04:55,133
Now put d x in a value tracker,

105
00:04:55,133 --> 00:04:59,139
and always redraw the secant, its
little triangle and its labels.

106
00:04:59,139 --> 00:05:02,912
As d x shrinks, the second
point slides toward the first,

107
00:05:02,912 --> 00:05:04,502
and the secant pivots.

108
00:05:04,502 --> 00:05:07,730
The slope readout
changes, and then settles.

109
00:05:07,730 --> 00:05:11,260
For this curve, x squared
over four plus one,

110
00:05:11,260 --> 00:05:16,298
at x equals one point five, it
settles at zero point seven five.

111
00:05:16,298 --> 00:05:20,443
The line it settles into touches
the curve at just that one point.

112
00:05:20,443 --> 00:05:24,181
It's the tangent line, and
its slope is the derivative.

113
00:05:24,181 --> 00:05:27,374
Again, the animation shows
you the limit happening,

114
00:05:27,374 --> 00:05:30,617
instead of asking you to imagine it.

115
00:05:30,617 --> 00:05:32,475
Plot draws graphs of functions:

116
00:05:32,475 --> 00:05:34,077
one height for each x.

117
00:05:34,077 --> 00:05:36,074
But many curves aren't like that.

118
00:05:36,074 --> 00:05:38,895
A circle has two heights
for most x values,

119
00:05:38,895 --> 00:05:39,951
above and below.

120
00:05:39,951 --> 00:05:42,773
For those, describe the curve
with a parameter instead.

121
00:05:42,773 --> 00:05:46,941
Plot parametric curve takes a function
of t that returns both coordinates,

122
00:05:46,941 --> 00:05:51,074
x and y, and traces the point
as t runs through a range.

123
00:05:51,074 --> 00:05:53,686
Cosine t and sine t give a circle.

124
00:05:53,686 --> 00:05:57,517
Cosine of three t and sine of
two t give this looping figure,

125
00:05:57,517 --> 00:06:01,615
called a Lissajous curve, which you
may have seen on an old oscilloscope.

126
00:06:01,615 --> 00:06:06,283
And to finish a graph, get axis labels
puts names at the ends of the axes.

127
00:06:06,283 --> 00:06:07,281
It's a small touch,

128
00:06:07,281 --> 00:06:11,850
but a graph without axis labels
is a picture without a caption.

129
00:06:11,850 --> 00:06:14,787
Graphs aren't the only thing
worth drawing on a grid.

130
00:06:14,787 --> 00:06:17,237
Sometimes the grid itself is the star.

131
00:06:17,237 --> 00:06:21,451
A linear transformation moves every point
of the plane in a way that keeps grid

132
00:06:21,451 --> 00:06:24,563
lines straight, parallel
and evenly spaced.

133
00:06:24,563 --> 00:06:27,814
And a two by two matrix
describes one completely.

134
00:06:27,814 --> 00:06:31,494
Its first column says where the
arrow one-to-the-right ends up.

135
00:06:31,494 --> 00:06:35,116
Its second column says where
the arrow one-upward ends up.

136
00:06:35,116 --> 00:06:36,929
Everything else follows along.

137
00:06:36,929 --> 00:06:40,399
Manim has a scene type
built for exactly this:

138
00:06:40,399 --> 00:06:42,094
linear transformation scene.

139
00:06:42,094 --> 00:06:45,345
It draws the grid and those
two basis arrows for you.

140
00:06:45,345 --> 00:06:48,201
Call apply matrix, and
the whole plane moves.

141
00:06:48,201 --> 00:06:52,067
This shear leaves the first arrow
alone and tips the second over to one,

142
00:06:52,067 --> 00:06:54,819
one. The yellow vector, one, two,

143
00:06:54,819 --> 00:06:56,653
rides along to three, two.

144
00:06:56,653 --> 00:06:59,416
Then a quarter-turn
rotation spins everything.

145
00:06:59,416 --> 00:07:01,936
If this way of seeing
matrices is new to you,

146
00:07:01,936 --> 00:07:03,573
it's worth slowing down for.

147
00:07:03,573 --> 00:07:05,279
Pause the video, scan the code,

148
00:07:05,279 --> 00:07:08,196
and try your own matrices.

149
00:07:11,150 --> 00:07:13,240
Axes give a scene its own ruler,

150
00:07:13,240 --> 00:07:16,746
and c to p converts graph
numbers into screen points.

151
00:07:16,746 --> 00:07:18,378
Plots are sampled curves.

152
00:07:18,378 --> 00:07:21,367
Riemann rectangles and
get area show integrals;

153
00:07:21,367 --> 00:07:23,445
a moving bound shows accumulation;

154
00:07:23,445 --> 00:07:26,742
a shrinking d x shows a
derivative being born.

155
00:07:26,742 --> 00:07:31,026
And linear transformation scene
shows what matrices do to space.

156
00:07:31,026 --> 00:07:33,859
So far, the camera has
stayed perfectly still.

157
00:07:33,859 --> 00:07:35,415
Next time, we move it:

158
00:07:35,415 --> 00:07:38,852
zoom into tiny details,
open a magnifying inset,

159
00:07:38,852 --> 00:07:41,847
and then tilt the whole
world into three dimensions.
